---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import QuoteForm from "../../components/QuoteForm.astro";
import { SITE } from "../../config";

const title = "How Long Does a Keratin Treatment Last? Timeline & Tips to Extend It";
const description =
  "How long keratin treatments actually last, month by month — and the factors (treatment type, hair, aftercare) that decide whether you're on the short or long end.";
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} path="/how-long-does-keratin-last/">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10 prose-section">
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-600 font-medium mb-2">
      <a href="/" class="hover:underline">Keratin Treatment Elmwood Park</a> / How Long Does Keratin Last?
    </p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-serif font-bold text-brand-900 mb-5">
      How Long Does a Keratin Treatment Last?
    </h1>
    <p>
      "2 to 5 months" is the answer you'll see everywhere, and it's accurate — but it's also a wide enough range
      that it's worth understanding exactly what pushes a treatment toward the short end versus the long end.
    </p>

    <h2>Baseline by Treatment Type</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Express keratin:</strong> typically 4–8 weeks — a lighter, faster service, often used as a maintenance touch-up between full treatments.</li>
      <li><strong>Brazilian blowout:</strong> typically 8–12 weeks, with the advantage of no downtime before washing.</li>
      <li><strong>Traditional / full keratin:</strong> typically 3–5 months — the longest-lasting mainstream option.</li>
      <li><strong>Nanoplasty / formaldehyde-free specialty:</strong> typically 2–4 months, varying by formula.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For current pricing across these same categories, see the
      <a href="/keratin-treatment-cost/" class="text-brand-600 font-medium">full cost guide →</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What Actually Moves You Within That Range</h2>
    <h3 class="font-serif text-lg font-semibold text-brand-900 mt-6 mb-1.5">Hair Porosity and Texture</h3>
    <p>
      More porous hair — often color-treated, previously chemically processed, or naturally coarse hair — can both
      absorb a treatment especially well and lose it a bit faster, since the same open cuticle structure that lets
      keratin in also lets it wash out more easily. Fine, less porous hair sometimes holds a treatment for a shorter
      window simply because there's less surface area and structure for the keratin to bond to.
    </p>
    <h3 class="font-serif text-lg font-semibold text-brand-900 mt-6 mb-1.5">Wash Frequency and Products</h3>
    <p>
      This is the single biggest controllable factor. Sulfate shampoos strip keratin out significantly faster than
      sulfate-free formulas, and washing daily fades a treatment faster than washing every other day. Two clients
      with the same treatment can see results last twice as long or half as long purely based on this. Full details
      in our <a href="/keratin-aftercare/" class="text-brand-600 font-medium">aftercare guide →</a>.
    </p>
    <h3 class="font-serif text-lg font-semibold text-brand-900 mt-6 mb-1.5">Heat Styling and Environmental Exposure</h3>
    <p>
      Daily high-heat styling without protection, chlorine from pools, and salt water from swimming all accelerate
      fade. Humid climates also mean the treatment is doing more "work" resisting frizz day to day, which can shorten
      its effective lifespan slightly compared to a drier climate — see our
      <a href="/keratin-for-frizz-humidity/" class="text-brand-600 font-medium">guide to keratin and humidity →</a>
      for more on this specifically.
    </p>

    <h2>A Rough Month-by-Month Timeline (Traditional Keratin)</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Weeks 1–4:</strong> Peak results — smoothest, shiniest, least frizz.</li>
      <li><strong>Weeks 5–10:</strong> Gradual softening; roots may start showing more natural texture as new growth comes in untreated.</li>
      <li><strong>Weeks 11–16:</strong> Noticeably more natural texture and frizz returning, especially in humidity; many clients book a touch-up around here.</li>
      <li><strong>Beyond 16 weeks:</strong> Treatment has mostly washed out for most clients; hair returns close to its natural state.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Express keratin and Brazilian blowout timelines compress this same pattern into a shorter window; nanoplasty
      and other formaldehyde-free formulas land somewhere in between, depending on the specific product.
    </p>

    <h2>When to Book a Touch-Up</h2>
    <p>
      Most stylists recommend booking a touch-up when you notice results softening rather than waiting until the
      treatment has fully faded — starting from a partially-treated base generally requires less product and time
      than starting completely from scratch.
    </p>

    <p>
      Want to know how a specific treatment type compares side by side? See
      <a href="/keratin-vs-brazilian-blowout/" class="text-brand-600 font-medium">Keratin vs. Brazilian Blowout →</a>
      or the <a href="/keratin-vs-nanoplasty/" class="text-brand-600 font-medium">Keratin vs. Nanoplasty →</a>
      comparison. Ready to book? <a href={`tel:${SITE.phone}`} class="text-brand-600 font-medium">Call {SITE.phoneDisplay}</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
  <section class="max-w-md mx-auto px-4 pb-16">
    <QuoteForm compact />
  </section>
</Layout>
